WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.800
Over the years I've been making videos,

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:05.880
a lot of people have got in touch
to tell me that in Warsaw, in Poland,

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:10.000
the tap water quality is tested
and controlled by clams.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:11.000
They said that the city

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:14.040
constantly runs a sample of its tap water
past some mollusks.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.960
And those are filter feeders,
so if there's a contaminant in the water,

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:18.640
they will instinctively close.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:22.080
Sensors attached to them
will detect that and raise the alarm.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:23.320
And I didn't believe it.

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:24.960
It seemed too weird to me.

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:29.040
And all the articles that I could find
about it seemed dodgy, somehow.

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:31.560
They just set off red flags
in the research parts of my brain.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:34.920
There is a very arty Polish documentary
about the system, and a lot of sites

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:37.360
repost that film’s claims
without even mentioning the source,

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:39.960
just as a “look at this
funny thing off in Poland!”.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:43.320
It felt like it could be a misinterpreted
or mistranslated joke,

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:46.320
or even a deliberate hoax by a filmmaker
that got out of control,

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:47.520
and that the English speaking world

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:51.000
was just believing it
because it was quirky and harmless enough.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.960
There have been plenty of fake
things like that spread about in the past,

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:56.880
accidentally or otherwise.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:59.840
Or maybe the clam-sensors did exist,
but it was a brief experiment

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:01.080
that never went anywhere.

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:04.640
Well, I'm now here in Warsaw,
on a famous building

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:07.760
in the middle of the river, with a Polish
speaking team showing me around.

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:11.480
There is a lot of stuff we are not allowed
to film here for security reasons.

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:14.080
Inside here is critical infrastructure.

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:16.640
Plus, it is far too noisy down there
for me to talk, but

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:19.080
I'm happy to report that yes,
the clams are real.

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:21.000
They actually do protect Warsaw,

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.040
but the story's a little more
complicated than that.

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:25.760
IN POLISH:

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:02.560
The important thing is: those clams aren't
the only protection.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:03.800
They're not even the main part.

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:06.240
This is still a modern
water treatment system,

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:09.440
with 21st century sensors and
detectors to keep everyone safe.

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:13.840
This is part of a defense in depth,
one part of an early warning system,

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:15.120
one more alarm that can go off,

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:17.960
like an airport that uses
explosive sniffing dogs...

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:19.880
but also electronic detectors.

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:53.960
The technical term
for this is “biomonitoring”,

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:57.240
and it turns out from more research that
this isn't the only place that does it.

00:03:57.240 --> 00:04:00.120
There are other water companies in Poland
that have similar setups,

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:03.480
plus one in Minnesota, USA,
and probably more that I haven't found.

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:08.000
At which point I think I can say that:
yes, the red flag in my head was wrong.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:11.360
The clams in this building
really do help protect Warsaw.

